UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 9, Lesson 3: Compound and complex sentences', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A compound sentence links two independent clauses, often with and, but or so. A complex sentence includes a clause that cannot stand alone, introduced by words such as because, although or when.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCombine ''Nia packed the lunch'' and ''the group left early'' using and, then express a reason using because.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCompound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible completion.\n\nCHECK\nJoining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite three compound and three complex sentences about an outdoor activity. Mark independent clauses, circle conjunctions, and explain why the because- or although-clause cannot stand alone.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"A compound sentence links two independent clauses, often with and, but or so. A complex sentence includes a clause that cannot stand alone, introduced by words such as because, although or when.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Language and spelling","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write three compound and three complex sentences about an outdoor activity. Mark independent clauses, circle conjunctions, and explain why the because- or although-clause cannot stand alone. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Compound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible completion. Reasoning: I used compound by attending to complex and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses. Correction: Compound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible… Why: the correction restores accurate use of compound.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating compound on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with compound, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Compound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible… This matches the lesson skill for compound.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Compound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible…","Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Combine ''Nia packed the lunch'' and ''the group left early'' using and, then express a reason using because. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Compound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible…","Careful use of complex and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses.","A compound sentence links two independent clauses, often with and, but or so. A complex sentence includes a clause that cannot stand alone, introduced by…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising compound?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named complex and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the complex and explain why it matters used."],"question":"A learner is practising compound. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries compound.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show compound and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Read: \"At dawn, Mina whispered to her goat as frost glittered on the fence.\" Which choice best uses compound from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Compound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible…","Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about compound?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise complex and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about compound.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of compound to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating compound on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Compound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible completion. Reasoning: I used compound by attending to complex and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with compound, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses. Correction: Compound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible… Why: the correction restores accurate use of compound.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w09-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise compound.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use compound with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify complex and explain why it matters.","I can practise conjunction independently on a new prompt.","I can notice clause needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A compound sentence links two independent clauses, often with and, but or so. A complex sentence includes a clause that cannot stand alone, introduced by words such as because, although or when. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the…","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","A compound sentence links two independent clauses, often with and, but or so. A complex sentence includes a clause that cannot stand alone, introduced by words such as because, although or when."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about compound?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCompound: ''The path was muddy, so we wore boots.'' Both halves can stand alone. Complex: ''We wore boots because the path was muddy.'' The ''because'' part needs the main clause to complete its meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCombine ''Nia packed the lunch'' and ''the group left early'' using and, then express a reason using because.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCompound: ''Nia packed the lunch, and the group left early.'' Complex: ''The group left early because the weather was changing.'' The new reason is one possible completion.","explain":"A compound sentence links two independent clauses, often with and, but or so. A complex sentence includes a clause that cannot stand alone, introduced by words such as because, although or when.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Joining any two fragments with and does not automatically create two independent clauses. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Compound so-sentence beside a complex because-sentence.","caption":"Complex clauses need the main clause to finish meaning.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Compound</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">muddy, so we wore boots</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Complex</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">because the path was muddy</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Compound: ''The path was muddy, so we wore boots.'' Both halves can stand alone. Complex: ''We wore boots because the path  Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Compound: ''The path was mudd